# Wolseley 16/20, 1910-1915.
#
# Wolseley's big four: 3,079 cc four, 90 x 121 mm, 20.1 RAC hp, four-speed
# gearbox from 1911, final drive worm or bevel depending on the year. A 1913
# contemporary account called it the most popular motor vehicle yet produced in
# a British factory. Overall dimensions were body-dependent and no works figure
# has been found; the catalogue records the chassis and engine, not the body.
#
# Contemporary estimates conflict: about 4,000 of the model were built in the
# year to November 1913, while Wolseley as a whole was credited with 3,000 cars
# in 1913, the largest British output. No total for the model has been found.
# https://doi.org/10.1136/bmj.2.2759.1308
# https://www.gracesguide.co.uk/1913_Automotive_Facts_and_Figures
# https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_Wolseley_automobiles
#
# Colours: Black, Dark Green, Maroon and Grey, as specified for this model.
#
# Weight: 13 in total (black 5, green 4, maroon 2, grey 2). Class 3 and a
# low-volume prestige car, so the total stays inside the 10-15 band.
#
# Speed 80 km/h: no published 16/20 maximum speed has been found. 80 km/h
# (50 mph) is the published maximum of the comparable 3,016 cc Sunbeam 12/16 at
# the National Motor Museum, used here as the nearest published figure and
# flagged for review. Comfort 60/270 (line). Class 3.
# Dates: 1910-1915 with a road tail to about 1940. The register said 1911-1919;
# the catalogues place the model in the range from 1910 to 1915, and the later
# date is not supported by any source found.
#
# Metres master: citycars/4.5/wolseley-16-20.blend. References:
# citycars/references/wolseley-16-20/ (gitignored). Images via Wikimedia
# Commons.
#
# Photographs: wolseley1620-a.jpg period side view, public domain,
# https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wolseley_16-20_open_touring_car.jpg
# wolseley1620-b.jpg 1913 tourer front, CC BY 2.0, Andrew Bone,
# https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1913_Wolseley_16-20_tourer)_(34615480686).jpg
# wolseley1620-c.jpg 1914 tourer front three-quarter, CC BY-SA 2.0, Clive Barker,
# https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1914_Wolseley_Tourer_(14771831790).jpg
# wolseley1620-d.jpg 1911 tourer front three-quarter, CC BY 2.0, Sicnag,
# https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1911_Wolseley_16-20_tourer_(42354972432).jpg

